{=============================================================================
  Shell-and-tube pressure drop: Kern and the Bell-Delaware correction factors
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0103 (function library, kind = function)
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Shell-side (crossflow) pressure drop of a shell-and-tube heat exchanger by the
  equivalent-diameter method of Kern, and the five correction factors of the
  Bell-Delaware method: baffle configuration Jc, baffle leakage Jl, bundle
  bypassing Jb, unequal baffle spacing Js and the laminar-flow correction Jr.

  dP_Kern returns the pressure drop across the tube bundle [Pa] from the mass
  flow rate, the fluid properties and the shell geometry. The five Bell-Delaware
  factors are dimensionless and multiply the shell-side heat-transfer and
  pressure-drop coefficients of the HEDH method; assembling them with the
  friction factor, the flow area and the geometric factor is left to the caller.

  Curve data: the friction factor of the Kern method is read from a graph; the
  cubic B-spline that ht fits to the digitized points is reproduced here in
  EES arithmetic (Cox-de Boor basis functions), so no lookup table is needed.
  The three Bell-Delaware curves of ht (Jc, Jl, Jb) are also digitized graphs;
  for those the closed forms that the original documents give are used - the
  Chebyshev fit of ht for Jc and the HEDH curve fits for Jl and Jb (see the
  comment block of each function and the README, "Conversion log").

  Not translated: dP_Zukauskas (Zukauskas 1972). Its friction factor and its
  pitch-ratio correction are bivariate splines of digitized graphs (104 to 228
  coefficients each, ht/conv_tube_bank.py), which have no closed form and are
  far too large to expand into EES equations; see README.md, "Limitations".

  The demonstration program at the end calls every function, once with the
  inputs of the ht doctest of the corresponding correlation ("A" cases) and
  once with a second input set ("B" cases). The ht values of both sets are
  tabulated in the README (verification table).

  Inputs : m [kg/s], rho [kg/m^3], mu / mu_w [Pa-s], DShell / LSpacing / pitch /
           Do [m], NBaffles [-], Ssb / Stb / Sm [m^2], fractions and counts [-],
           Re [-], laminar / method flags [-]
  Outputs: dP [Pa]; Jc, Jl, Jb, Js, Jr [-] (one FUNCTION each)
  Units  : SI - masses in kg, lengths in m, pressures in Pa
  Source : ht (Caleb Bell), ht/conv_tube_bank.py, version 1.2.0, commit 85e0ee6,
           MIT - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht ; correlations by the authors
           cited in each function block - see README.md
  Status : VERIFIED against the doctests and the values of ht 1.2.0
           (README.md, "Verification")
=============================================================================}

FUNCTION dP_Kern(m, rho, mu, mu_w, DShell, LSpacing, pitch, Do, NBaffles)
{DP_KERN - pressure drop across the tube bundle of a shell-and-tube heat
exchanger, equivalent-diameter method of Kern; f is the friction factor read
from the graph of Kern (as in Peters and Timmerhaus). Pass mu_w = mu for the
form without the wall-viscosity correction (ht makes mu_w optional).

  dP = f*(m/Ss)^2*DShell*(NBaffles+1) / {2*rho*De*(mu/mu_w)^0.14}

  Ss  = DShell*(pitch-Do)*LSpacing/pitch          "shell-side flow area [m^2]"
  De  = 4*(pitch^2 - pi*Do^2/4)/(pi*Do)           "equivalent diameter [m]"
  Vs  = m/Ss/rho                                  "velocity in the crossflow
                                                    section [m/s]"
  Re  = rho*De*Vs/mu                              "Reynolds number on De [-]"

Validity: none quoted by ht; the graph is scanned from Kern and the friction
factor is valid on the range of its spline, Re = 9.95 to 1.012E6 [-].
Original: Kern, Donald Quentin, Process Heat Transfer, McGraw-Hill, 1950;
  presented in Peters, Max, Klaus Timmerhaus and Ronald West, Plant Design and
  Economics for Chemical Engineers, 5E, McGraw-Hill, New York, 2002 (the doctest
  example, about 10% difference due to reading the graph).
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_tube_bank.py, dP_Kern,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Ss = DShell*(pitch - Do)*LSpacing/pitch
  De = 4*(pitch^2 - pi()*Do^2/4)/(pi()*Do)
  Vs = m/Ss/rho
  Re = rho*De*Vs/mu

  // Cox-de Boor basis functions of the cubic B-spline of ht (15 knots, 11 non-zero
  // coefficients): it reproduces the friction factor f(Re) read from the graph of
  // Kern. Knots: 9.9524 (x4), 17.9105, 27.7862, 47.2083, 83.9573, 281.996,
  // 1122.76, 42999.9, 1012440 (x4).
  // Zero-length knot intervals (repeated knots) have an identically zero term in
  // the recursion and are dropped, which leaves the equations below unchanged.
  B0_3 = Kern_knot_step(Re, 9.9524, 17.9105)
  B0_4 = Kern_knot_step(Re, 17.9105, 27.7862)
  B0_5 = Kern_knot_step(Re, 27.7862, 47.2083)
  B0_6 = Kern_knot_step(Re, 47.2083, 83.9573)
  B0_7 = Kern_knot_step(Re, 83.9573, 281.996)
  B0_8 = Kern_knot_step(Re, 281.996, 1122.76)
  B0_9 = Kern_knot_step(Re, 1122.76, 42999.9)
  B0_10 = Kern_knot_step(Re, 42999.9, 1012440)

  B1_0 = 0
  B1_1 = 0
  B1_2 = (17.9105 - Re)/7.9581*B0_3
  B1_3 = (Re - 9.9524)/7.9581*B0_3 + (27.7862 - Re)/9.8757*B0_4
  B1_4 = (Re - 17.9105)/9.8757*B0_4 + (47.2083 - Re)/19.4221*B0_5
  B1_5 = (Re - 27.7862)/19.4221*B0_5 + (83.9573 - Re)/36.749*B0_6
  B1_6 = (Re - 47.2083)/36.749*B0_6 + (281.996 - Re)/198.0387*B0_7
  B1_7 = (Re - 83.9573)/198.0387*B0_7 + (1122.76 - Re)/840.764*B0_8
  B1_8 = (Re - 281.996)/840.764*B0_8 + (42999.9 - Re)/41877.14*B0_9
  B1_9 = (Re - 1122.76)/41877.14*B0_9 + (1012440 - Re)/969440.1*B0_10
  B1_10 = (Re - 42999.9)/969440.1*B0_10
  B1_11 = 0
  B1_12 = 0

  B2_0 = 0
  B2_1 = (17.9105 - Re)/7.9581*B1_2
  B2_2 = (Re - 9.9524)/7.9581*B1_2 + (27.7862 - Re)/17.8338*B1_3
  B2_3 = (Re - 9.9524)/17.8338*B1_3 + (47.2083 - Re)/29.2978*B1_4
  B2_4 = (Re - 17.9105)/29.2978*B1_4 + (83.9573 - Re)/56.1711*B1_5
  B2_5 = (Re - 27.7862)/56.1711*B1_5 + (281.996 - Re)/234.7877*B1_6
  B2_6 = (Re - 47.2083)/234.7877*B1_6 + (1122.76 - Re)/1038.8027*B1_7
  B2_7 = (Re - 83.9573)/1038.8027*B1_7 + (42999.9 - Re)/42717.904*B1_8
  B2_8 = (Re - 281.996)/42717.904*B1_8 + (1012440 - Re)/1011317.24*B1_9
  B2_9 = (Re - 1122.76)/1011317.24*B1_9 + (1012440 - Re)/969440.1*B1_10
  B2_10 = (Re - 42999.9)/969440.1*B1_10
  B2_11 = 0

  B3_0 = (17.9105 - Re)/7.9581*B2_1
  B3_1 = (Re - 9.9524)/7.9581*B2_1 + (27.7862 - Re)/17.8338*B2_2
  B3_2 = (Re - 9.9524)/17.8338*B2_2 + (47.2083 - Re)/37.2559*B2_3
  B3_3 = (Re - 9.9524)/37.2559*B2_3 + (83.9573 - Re)/66.0468*B2_4
  B3_4 = (Re - 17.9105)/66.0468*B2_4 + (281.996 - Re)/254.2098*B2_5
  B3_5 = (Re - 27.7862)/254.2098*B2_5 + (1122.76 - Re)/1075.5517*B2_6
  B3_6 = (Re - 47.2083)/1075.5517*B2_6 + (42999.9 - Re)/42915.9427*B2_7
  B3_7 = (Re - 83.9573)/42915.9427*B2_7 + (1012440 - Re)/1012158.004*B2_8
  B3_8 = (Re - 281.996)/1012158.004*B2_8 + (1012440 - Re)/1011317.24*B2_9
  B3_9 = (Re - 1122.76)/1011317.24*B2_9 + (1012440 - Re)/969440.1*B2_10
  B3_10 = (Re - 42999.9)/969440.1*B2_10

  f_Kern = 6.04043594917824*B3_0 + 4.64973456285782*B3_1 + 2.95274850806163*B3_2 + 1.9569061885042*B3_3 + 1.16630699464204*B3_4 + 0.68305495362151*B3_5 + 0.458868026544776*B3_6 + 0.223877923319717*B3_7 + 0.127211909755306*B3_8 + 0.139545654888124*B3_9 + 0.128888957434687*B3_10

  dP_Kern = f_Kern*(Vs*rho)^2*DShell*(NBaffles + 1)/(2*rho*De*(mu/mu_w)^0.14)
END

FUNCTION Kern_knot_step(x, a, b)
{KERN_KNOT_STEP - helper of dP_Kern, not a correlation: 1 if the half-open knot
interval [a, b[ contains x, 0 otherwise. It is the degree-0 function of the
Cox-de Boor recursion of the friction-factor spline of Kern. The block IF form
is used because the single-line form of the conditional is mis-evaluated by
CoolSolve 0.3.0 (see the README, "Limitations and CoolSolve gaps").
  Kern_knot_step = 1 if a <= x < b, 0 otherwise}
  IF x >= a THEN
    IF x < b THEN
      Kern_knot_step = 1
    ELSE
      Kern_knot_step = 0
    ENDIF
  ELSE
    Kern_knot_step = 0
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION Jc_Bell(Fc, method)
{JC_BELL - baffle configuration (window) correction factor of the Bell-Delaware
method: it accounts for the tubes that are in the baffle window, where the
fluid flows parallel to them, and not in crossflow. Fc is the fraction of the
tubes between baffle tips (1 = no tube in the window).

  Jc = 0.55 + 0.72*Fc                        (method = 2, HEDH linear curve fit)

  method = 1: Chebyshev fit of the digitized graph of Bell,
             Jc = sum(c_k*(2*Fc - 1)^k), k = 0..16, evaluated in Horner form
             (the maximum error of this fit is 0.142% and its average error
              0.04%, i.e. within the error of the digitization - as in ht)

Validity: 0 <= Fc <= 1 [-]. ht notes max ~1.1536 at ~0.9066, min ~0.5328 at
  Fc = 0 and ~1.0314 at Fc = 1; well designed exchangers have Jc near 1.0.
Original: Bell, Kenneth J., Final Report of the Cooperative Research Program on
  Shell and Tube Heat Exchangers, University of Delaware, Engineering
  Experimental Station, 1963; Bell, Delaware Method for Shell-Side Design, in
  Shah, R. K., Eleswarapu Chinna Subbarao and R. A. Mashelkar (eds), Heat
  Transfer Equipment Design, CRC Press, 1988; the linear fit is given in
  Schlunder, E. U. (ed.), Heat Exchanger Design Handbook, Hemisphere, 1987 and
  in Serth, R. W., Process Heat Transfer, 2E, Academic Press, 2014.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_tube_bank.py, baffle_correction_Bell
  (the 'chebyshev' and the 'HEDH' methods; the default 'spline' method is a
  digitized curve and is not translated), MIT License, C. Bell}
  IF method = 1 THEN
    u = 2*Fc - 1
    Jc_Bell = -17.2670875309741
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u - 17.3410726763777
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u + 60.3838026259099
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u + 60.782028038612
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u - 83.865563269877
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u - 84.7402441123631
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u + 58.6646184487256
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u + 59.5614608259622
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u - 21.7869575471309
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u - 22.2293787075981
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u + 4.1167302227508
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u + 4.22624601250434
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u - 0.334972300460048
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u - 0.368582665326309
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u - 0.0629839069257099
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u + 0.358833096309762
    Jc_Bell = Jc_Bell*u + 0.934547858287335
  ELSE
    Jc_Bell = 0.55 + 0.72*Fc
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION Jl_Bell(Ssb, Stb, Sm)
{JL_BELL - baffle leakage factor of the Bell-Delaware method: it accounts for
the leakage between each baffle. Ssb is the shell-to-baffle leakage area, Stb
the total baffle leakage area and Sm the crossflow area.

  Jl = 0.44*(1 - r_s) + [1 - 0.44*(1 - r_s)]*exp(-2.2*r_lm)

  r_s  = Ssb/(Ssb + Stb)                       "shell-to-baffle share [-]"
  r_lm = (Ssb + Stb)/Sm                        "leakage-to-crossflow area
                                                 ratio [-]"

Validity: the HEDH curve fit quoted by ht; 0 <= Ssb/(Ssb + Stb) <= 1 (ht raises
  an error outside). The default method of ht interpolates the digitized graph
  (its argument is clipped at 0.743614); this closed form needs no clipping.
Original: Bell 1963 and 1988 (as above); the curve fit is given in Schlunder,
  E. U. (ed.), Heat Exchanger Design Handbook, Hemisphere, 1987 and in Serth,
  R. W., Process Heat Transfer, 2E, Academic Press, 2014.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_tube_bank.py, baffle_leakage_Bell
  (the 'HEDH' method; the default 'spline' method is a digitized curve and is
  not translated), MIT License, C. Bell}
  r_s = Ssb/(Ssb + Stb)
  r_lm = (Ssb + Stb)/Sm
  Jl_Bell = 0.44*(1 - r_s) + (1 - 0.44*(1 - r_s))*exp(-2.2*r_lm)
END

FUNCTION Jb_Bell(Fsbp, n_seal_strips, n_crossflow_rows, laminar)
{JB_BELL - bundle bypassing effect correction factor of the Bell-Delaware
method. Fsbp is the fraction of the crossflow area which is not blocked by a
baffle and is available for bypassing, n_seal_strips the number of seal strips
per side of a baffle and n_crossflow_rows the number of tube rows in the
crossflow of the baffle. laminar = 1 selects the laminar-flow coefficients.

  Jb = exp{-c*Fsbp*[1 - (2*z)^(1/3)]}           c = 1.35 (laminar), 1.25
                                               z = n_seal_strips/n_crossflow_rows

Validity: the HEDH curve fit quoted by ht; Fsbp is clipped at 0.695 in the
  default (spline) method of ht, this closed form needs no clipping.
Original: Bell 1963 and 1988 (as above); the curve fit is given in Schlunder,
  E. U. (ed.), Heat Exchanger Design Handbook, Hemisphere, 1987.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_tube_bank.py, bundle_bypassing_Bell
  (the 'HEDH' method; the default 'spline' method is a digitized curve and is
  not translated), MIT License, C. Bell}
  IF laminar = 1 THEN
    c = 1.35
  ELSE
    c = 1.25
  ENDIF
  z = n_seal_strips/n_crossflow_rows
  Jb_Bell = exp(-c*Fsbp*(1 - (2*z)^(1/3)))
END

FUNCTION Js_Bell(nb, B, B_in, B_out, laminar)
{JS_BELL - unequal baffle spacing correction factor of the Bell-Delaware method:
the inlet and/or outlet baffle spacing is often smaller than the standard
spacing B, so the fluid velocity and the heat-transfer coefficients are higher
there. nb is the number of baffles. laminar = 1 selects the laminar exponent.

  Js = {(nb - 1) + (B_in/B)^(1-n) + (B_out/B)^(1-n)}
       / {(nb - 1) + (B_in/B) + (B_out/B)}

  n = 1/3 (laminar), 0.6 (turbulent)

Validity: none quoted by ht. Pass B_in = B and B_out = B for equal spacing
  (ht makes these two arguments optional and then uses B).
Original: Bell 1963 and 1988 (as above).
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_tube_bank.py,
  unequal_baffle_spacing_Bell, MIT License, C. Bell}
  IF laminar = 1 THEN
    n = 1/3
  ELSE
    n = 0.6
  ENDIF
  Js_Bell = ((nb - 1) + (B_in/B)^(1 - n) + (B_out/B)^(1 - n))/((nb - 1) + B_in/B + B_out/B)
END

FUNCTION Jr_Bell(Re, total_row_passes)
{JR_BELL - correction factor for the adverse temperature gradient built up in
laminar flow (Bell-Delaware method). total_row_passes is the total number of
tube rows passed by the fluid, windows included and repeat passes of tube rows
counted.

  Jr* = (10/total_row_passes)^0.18

  Jr = 1                                if Re > 100
  Jr = Jr*                              if Re < 20
  Jr = Jr* + (20 - Re)/80*(Jr* - 1)      if 20 <= Re <= 100

  and the result is clipped from below at 0.4.

Validity: the Bell-Delaware method uses a Reynolds-number criterion of 100 for
  the laminar correction (as in ht).
Original: Bell 1963 and 1988 (as above); the interpolation between Re = 20 and
  Re = 100 and the 0.4 limit are described in Schlunder, E. U. (ed.), Heat
  Exchanger Design Handbook, Hemisphere, 1987 and in Serth, R. W., Process Heat
  Transfer, 2E, Academic Press, 2014.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_tube_bank.py, laminar_correction_Bell,
  MIT License, C. Bell}
  IF Re > 100 THEN
    Jr_Bell = 1
  ELSE
    Jr_star = (10/total_row_passes)^0.18
    IF Re < 20 THEN
      Jr_Bell = Jr_star
    ELSE
      Jr_Bell = Jr_star + (20 - Re)/80*(Jr_star - 1)
    ENDIF
    IF Jr_Bell < 0.4 THEN
      Jr_Bell = 0.4
    ENDIF
  ENDIF
END


"!Demonstration program: every correlation is called twice"
"It is ignored by the future $INCLUDE library:tube_bank_dp_bell_delaware (CS-FEAT-IMPORT)."
"Case A: the input set of the ht doctest of each correlation (published values)."
"Case B: a second input set, computed with the same Python functions."
"Both cases are tabulated against ht in the README (verification table)."

"Case A - input set"
m_A = 11              "mass flow rate [kg/s] (ht doctest of dP_Kern)"
rho_A = 995           "density of the shell-side fluid [kg/m^3]"
mu_A = 0.000803       "viscosity of the shell-side fluid [Pa-s]"
mu_w_A = 0.000657     "viscosity at the tube wall temperature [Pa-s]"
DShell_A = 0.584      "shell diameter [m]"
LSpacing_A = 0.1524   "baffle spacing [m]"
pitch_A = 0.0254      "tube pitch [m]"
Do_A = 0.019          "tube outside diameter [m]"
NBaffles_A = 22       "number of baffles [-]"
Fc_A = 0.82           "fraction of the tubes in crossflow (between baffle tips) [-]"
Ssb_A = 1             "shell-to-baffle leakage area [m^2]"
Stb_A = 3             "total baffle leakage area [m^2]"
Sm_A = 8              "crossflow area [m^2]"
Fsbp_A = 0.5          "bypass area fraction [-]"
n_seal_A = 5          "seal strips per baffle side [-]"
n_rows_A = 25         "tube rows in the baffle crossflow [-]"
nb_A = 16             "number of baffles [-]"
B_A = 0.1             "standard baffle spacing [m]"
B_in_A = 0.15         "inlet baffle spacing [m]"
B_out_A = 0.15        "outlet baffle spacing [m]"
Re_A = 30             "shell Reynolds number of the Bell-Delaware method [-]"
n_pass_A = 80         "total number of tube rows passed by the fluid [-]"

"Case A - calls"
dP_Kern_A = dP_Kern(m_A, rho_A, mu_A, mu_w_A, DShell_A, LSpacing_A, pitch_A, Do_A, NBaffles_A)
dP_Kern_equalmu_A = dP_Kern(m_A, rho_A, mu_A, mu_A, DShell_A, LSpacing_A, pitch_A, Do_A, NBaffles_A)
"without the wall-viscosity correction (mu_w = mu)"
Jc_Bell_A = Jc_Bell(Fc_A, 1)     "Chebyshev fit of the digitized graph of ht"
Jc_Bell_HEDH_A = Jc_Bell(Fc_A, 2)  "HEDH linear curve fit"
Jl_Bell_A = Jl_Bell(Ssb_A, Stb_A, Sm_A)
Jb_Bell_A = Jb_Bell(Fsbp_A, n_seal_A, n_rows_A, 0)
Jb_Bell_laminar_A = Jb_Bell(Fsbp_A, n_seal_A, n_rows_A, 1)
Js_Bell_A = Js_Bell(nb_A, B_A, B_in_A, B_out_A, 0)
Js_Bell_equal_A = Js_Bell(nb_A, B_A, B_A, B_A, 0)  "equal baffle spacings"
Js_Bell_laminar_A = Js_Bell(nb_A, B_A, B_in_A, B_out_A, 1)
Jr_Bell_A = Jr_Bell(Re_A, n_pass_A)
Jr_Bell_turbulent_A = Jr_Bell(150, n_pass_A)  "Re > 100: the factor is 1"

"Case B - second input set"
"!Oil side of a 1-shell-pass exchanger: 8 kg/s of a light oil (rho = 82 kg/m^3,
!mu = 2.4E-5 Pa-s, mu_w = 3.1E-5 Pa-s), 600 mm shell, 200 mm baffle spacing,
!32 mm tube pitch, 25 mm tubes, 16 baffles; leakage areas in m^2, a bypass
!fraction of 0.12 and a laminar shell Reynolds number of 60."
m_B = 8
rho_B = 82
mu_B = 0.000024
mu_w_B = 0.000031
DShell_B = 0.6
LSpacing_B = 0.25
pitch_B = 0.032
Do_B = 0.025
NBaffles_B = 16
Fc_B = 0.85
Ssb_B = 0.004
Stb_B = 0.012
Sm_B = 0.08
Fsbp_B = 0.12
n_seal_B = 4
n_rows_B = 20
nb_B = 12
B_B = 0.2
B_in_B = 0.28
B_out_B = 0.18
Re_B = 60
n_pass_B = 40

"Case B - calls"
dP_Kern_B = dP_Kern(m_B, rho_B, mu_B, mu_w_B, DShell_B, LSpacing_B, pitch_B, Do_B, NBaffles_B)
dP_Kern_equalmu_B = dP_Kern(m_B, rho_B, mu_B, mu_B, DShell_B, LSpacing_B, pitch_B, Do_B, NBaffles_B)
Jc_Bell_B = Jc_Bell(Fc_B, 1)
Jc_Bell_HEDH_B = Jc_Bell(Fc_B, 2)
Jl_Bell_B = Jl_Bell(Ssb_B, Stb_B, Sm_B)
Jb_Bell_B = Jb_Bell(Fsbp_B, n_seal_B, n_rows_B, 0)
Jb_Bell_laminar_B = Jb_Bell(Fsbp_B, n_seal_B, n_rows_B, 1)
Js_Bell_B = Js_Bell(nb_B, B_B, B_in_B, B_out_B, 0)
Js_Bell_equal_B = Js_Bell(nb_B, B_B, B_B, B_B, 0)
Js_Bell_laminar_B = Js_Bell(nb_B, B_B, B_in_B, B_out_B, 1)
Jr_Bell_B = Jr_Bell(Re_B, n_pass_B)

